$DIR/main.p:16:3: cannot store e in cache[k], which outlives get's frame
    e points into a composite literal in get's frame. A composite literal whose
    address is taken (at main.p:15) is made in get's frame, as a local variable
    would be. When get returns, its frame is reclaimed, but a map keeps its keys
    and elements in a table on the heap, which every copy of the map shares and
    which may live longer, so it would be left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value itself instead of a view of get's frame, changing the map's
    element type to match.
    Or keep what the map refers to in memory that lives as long as the map, such
    as a package variable, or in a counted reference (shared[T] or synced[T])
    stored in the map.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
